# tools/al/ales_stereo_voiced_witness.rish — stereo_voiced: the voiced/unvoiced/silent classifier carried into stereo with ONE SHARED BAND/FLOOR/SPLIT and TWO INDEPENDENT VERDICTS, the suite's second stereo analysis rung (ALES146).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES146.
# Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-045940_fill-ales146-lotus-stereo-voiced.md
# Stands on: lotus/voiced.rye (ALES94 — the mono voiced/unvoiced/silent classifier run once per channel, its classify, its Verdict, its ClassifyError, all reused whole), lotus/schmitt.rye (ALES92 — the band's own precheck,
# the band-and-span law), lotus/meter.rye (ALES13 — sample_peak_max, the floor ceiling, and the RMS energy the classifier fuses), lotus/zero_cross.rye (ALES93 — the crossing count the classifier fuses), lotus/stereo_zero_cross.rye
# (ALES145 — the stereo analysis shape this mirrors, one shared setting with per-channel independence), lotus/pan.rye (ALES10 — the StereoClip, two mono Clips left/right, its balance invariant), lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2 — the
# Clip, its buf/len/samples). A Lotus master is a StereoClip whose defining invariant is that left and right hold the same length. Where ALES145 carried the single crossing-count reader into stereo, this rung carries the reader
# that FUSES two of them — energy (RMS) separating sound from silence, crossing rate separating the two kinds of sound above the floor: two classifiers reading two channels, each returning a three-valued verdict, writing not
# one sample. Each channel is classified on its own two features, so the two verdicts are independent — a loud-periodic left over a quiet right reads voiced / silent, an identical master reads equal verdicts on both.
#
# lotus/stereo_voiced.rye validates the shared band, span, and floor once before either channel is read (BadThreshold on an illegal band, BadRange on a span outside the samples, BadFloor on a floor above the peak ceiling), then
# classifies both channels through ALES94's classify; ClassifyError reused whole. The crux is four laws. THE STEREO FUSION LAW: each channel's verdict equals ALES94's mono classify with the same band, floor, and split. THE
# READ-ONLY / BALANCE LAW: the source StereoClip byte for byte unchanged on both, the pair balanced. THE INDEPENDENT-VERDICT LAW: a loud-periodic left over a quiet right reading voiced / silent, an identical master reading equal
# verdicts, the energy gate first per channel. THE ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW: a shared BadFloor / BadThreshold / BadRange refusing before either channel is classified with the clip untouched, the floor-zero convention on both.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/al/ales_stereo_voiced_witness.rish

say "ales-stereo-voiced: Language EN — stereo_voiced classifies a span in both channels of a stereo master as silent, voiced, or unvoiced through one shared band, floor, and split, returning the pair of per-channel verdicts and writing not one sample. A Lotus master is two Clips heard together, one per speaker, whose defining invariant is that left and right hold the same length. This rung carries the suite's fusing analysis rung into stereo on ALES94's classifier, running the proven mono classifier on each channel with the same setting, validated once against the shared band, span, and floor before either channel is read so a refusal never classifies one channel and returns a half answer."
say "ales-stereo-voiced: Style Radiant — where ALES145 carried the single crossing-count reader into stereo, two counters reading two channels, this rung carries the reader that fuses two of them into one verdict: the oldest decision in speech processing, is this frame voiced, unvoiced, or silent, answered from exactly two features. Energy, ALES13's RMS, separates sound from silence, below a floor there is nothing to classify; crossing rate, ALES93's count read through the ALES92 band, separates the two kinds of sound above that floor, a voiced sound crossing few times and an unvoiced sound crossing many. The rule, energy gate first: rms below the floor is silent whatever the crossing rate, else many crossings is unvoiced, else voiced. It mirrors the stereo meter's own shape and the stereo zero-cross, two mono readers with per-channel results, and since a verdict is a pure function of one span it offers one form, classify_stereo, returning the pair. The honest signature of a stereo analysis rung is that each channel is classified on its own two features, so the two verdicts advance independently: an identical-channel master gives equal verdicts, a mono-in-stereo signal reading the same on both, while a loud-periodic left over a quiet right reads voiced on the left and silent on the right, the energy gate read first on each. The crux is the fusion rule carried up one level, in stereo: ALES94's classify invents no new measurement, its verdict equalling a decision built by hand from count_crossings and a meter's RMS over the same span, so each channel's stereo verdict equals ALES94's mono classify, and the stereo classifier can never drift from the mono classifier, which can never drift from its two readers. The rule is stated positively: the classifier reads only, the source clip leaving byte for byte unchanged on both channels, each verdict a three-valued label over one span, not a phoneme or a transcript. It reuses ALES94's ClassifyError by name, ALES94's classifier for the verdict, and ALES92's precheck for the band-and-span law, inventing no new fault and no new primitive. The crux stands out as four laws: the stereo fusion law, the read-only / balance law, the independent-verdict law (a loud-periodic left over a quiet right reading voiced / silent, an identical master reading equal verdicts, the energy gate first per channel), and the atomicity / degenerate law (a shared BadFloor / BadThreshold / BadRange refusing before either channel is classified with the clip untouched, the floor-zero convention holding on both)."
say "ales-stereo-voiced: Lens TAME — two bounded i16 Clips (left, right) read in lockstep by one shared band, floor, and split; the balance invariant is asserted on entry and exit, the shared band and span are bound-checked once via ALES92's precheck and the floor once against meter's sample_peak_max before any read, and because those checks hold identically for both balanced channels each mono classify is pre-validated to succeed so no refusal can land between the two channel verdicts; the classifier composes ALES94's classify per channel, which fuses ALES13's RMS and ALES93's count over the same span reading its features without writing a sample; the stereo fusion law is proven against ALES94's own classify run per channel across four floors and three splits, the independent-verdict law proves a loud-periodic left reads voiced while a quiet right reads silent and an identical master reads equal verdicts, the energy-gate-first ordering is proven per channel (a quiet many-crossing channel reads silent), the read-only law proves the source clip is byte-for-byte unchanged on both, and the atomicity cases leave the clip untouched with the floor-zero convention voiced on both and the span discipline reading only [start, count); siloed to lotus/, read-only, no adaptive floor, no time windowing, no real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no device, no speaker."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "lotus/bin/stereo_voiced"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p lotus/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/stereo_voiced.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "ales-stereo-voiced: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "lotus/bin/stereo_voiced.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "ales-stereo-voiced: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "ales-stereo-voiced: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN ales-stereo-voiced' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "ales-stereo-voiced: selftest did not print the GREEN ales-stereo-voiced line"
